finish('vp-versus-vbp-over-complex-numbers',
 id='TCS-6612', title='VP versus VBP over the complex numbers',
 area='Algebraic computation', criterion='resources', question_type='yes_no',
 formal='Is VP_ℂ=VBP_ℂ? Explicitly, for every family (f_n)_{n≥1} of commutative polynomials over ℂ with a polynomially bounded number of variables and total degree, if f_n has an arithmetic circuit of size n^O(1), must f_n also have an algebraic branching program of size n^O(1)? The polynomial bounds may depend on the family but not on n; all equalities are exact polynomial identities.',
 definitions='An arithmetic circuit is a finite directed acyclic graph with one output, input gates labelled by variables or arbitrary complex constants, and binary addition or multiplication gates; subtraction is expressible using the constant −1. Intermediate results may be reused without restriction. Size counts gates and wires, with each field constant of unit cost; no division gates are allowed. An algebraic branching program (ABP) is a finite directed acyclic graph with a source s and sink t, whose edges are labelled by affine linear forms α₀+∑_j α_j x_j over ℂ. Its output is the sum over all directed s-to-t paths of the product of their edge labels. Its size counts vertices, edges and the nonzero coefficients and variable occurrences used in those labels. Layering can be imposed with polynomial overhead but is not required here. There is no width, variable-order, read-once, multilinearity, homogeneity, monotonicity or symmetry restriction. Both circuit and ABP families are nonuniform: no efficient algorithm producing them is required, and complex constants need not have bounded bit descriptions. VP_ℂ consists of the stated polynomial-degree families with polynomial circuit size; VBP_ℂ consists of families with polynomial ABP size and polynomially many variables. Polynomial ABP size already bounds the degree.',
 answer_criterion='Prove that every family in VP_ℂ admits polynomial-size ABPs, or exhibit a family in VP_ℂ whose minimum ABP size is not polynomially bounded: for every constant c, infinitely many n require more than n^c size. A negative answer must retain small general circuits for the separating family and allow arbitrary complex constants in the competing ABPs. Lower bounds only for multilinear, symmetric, noncommutative, monotone or read-once ABPs do not suffice. Approximation in coefficient space, called border complexity, is a different question.',
 context=[
  paragraph('A circuit can multiply two complicated intermediate results and reuse each of them elsewhere. An ABP has a more linear form of reuse: the polynomial reaching a vertex is obtained by adding predecessor polynomials multiplied by affine edge labels. This is equivalent, with polynomial overhead, to skew circuits, where each multiplication has an input operand that is a variable or constant. The problem asks whether general multiplication and reuse can always be reorganized into this form at only polynomial cost.','primer'),
  paragraph('The degree restriction rules out an immediate artificial separation. Repeated squaring computes x^{2ⁿ} with n multiplication gates. An ABP with affine labels needs a path of length at least 2ⁿ to produce that degree. This family is excluded from VP because its degree is exponential. The open problem persists when the output degree is polynomial, so a solution must address the structure of efficient computations rather than this simple degree obstruction.'),
  paragraph('The determinant provides a useful equivalent perspective. Polynomial-size ABPs can be represented by determinants of polynomial-size matrices of affine forms, and the symbolic determinant itself has polynomial-size ABPs. Thus equality would make the determinant universal for all polynomial-degree, polynomial-size circuit computations under polynomial-size affine projections. This differs from the determinant-versus-permanent problem: the permanent is not known to belong to VP, whereas a separation here must already have an efficient circuit on the easy side.','primer'),
  paragraph('Classical depth reduction supplies a quasipolynomial simulation. If quasipolynomial size is allowed throughout, formulas, ABPs and general circuits compute the same polynomial-degree families. Dutta and coauthors recall this consequence of the Valiant–Skyum–Berkowitz–Rackoff depth reduction in their ITCS 2024 work. What remains unknown is whether the overhead can always be reduced to a fixed polynomial, or whether some efficient circuit computations necessarily resist it.','depth'),
  paragraph('ABPs also support more algebra than their restricted multiplication rule might suggest. Sinhababu and Thierauf prove that factors of an ABP-computed polynomial have polynomial-size ABPs in characteristic zero. This establishes a closure property that was previously known for general circuits. Sharing factor closure does not establish equality of the models; the June 2026 factoring survey explicitly retains the unresolved chain VF⊆VBP⊆VP.','factors'),
  paragraph('Dwivedi, Pago and Seppelt’s STOC 2026 work separates symmetric analogues of formulas, skew circuits and general circuits and connects them to graph homomorphism polynomials. These analogues require symmetries of the variables to extend to the computation and measure the largest orbit of gates, rather than simply imposing the ordinary total-size bound. Their separations therefore do not establish VP≠VBP. The work also supplies natural complete polynomial families, potentially useful targets for future unrestricted lower bounds.','symmetry'),
  paragraph('A January 2026 paper by Fabris, Limaye, Srinivasan and Yehudayoff investigates the min-partition rank method for multilinear ABPs through a combinatorial set-system problem. It recovers a separation between multilinear formulas and multilinear ABPs and studies the potential of the method for stronger lower bounds. The general ABPs in this card can reuse variables along paths and can cancel nonmultilinear terms. A lower bound that forbids those behaviors does not constrain every allowed representation.','multilinear'),
  paragraph('A positive answer would show that general efficient algebraic computation can be expressed through path sums and determinants with only polynomial overhead. A negative answer would prove a basic hierarchy inside efficient computation itself. Either result would clarify how much computational power comes from multiplying and reusing rich intermediate expressions, independently of the larger question of whether permanent computation is efficient.')],
 why='One of the central unresolved separations inside algebraic complexity: it asks whether determinant-like path-sum computation captures every efficiently computed polynomial of polynomial degree.',
 importance=dict(score=97,method='editorial',assessed_on='2026-09-10',reason='A basic class equality linking general arithmetic circuits, algebraic branching programs and determinant universality; current symmetry and multilinearity results remain model-specific.'),
 progress=[
  step('1983','Valiant–Skyum–Berkowitz–Rackoff depth reduction yields quasipolynomial simulations; the polynomial-size equality remains stronger. The ITCS 2024 paper reviews this consequence.','depth'),
  step('2020–2021','Sinhababu–Thierauf establish polynomial-size factor closure for ABPs in characteristic zero, first in CCC 2020 and then in Computational Complexity.','factors'),
  step('2026-01-01','Fabris–Limaye–Srinivasan–Yehudayoff study the power of min-partition rank for multilinear ABPs through combinatorial set systems.','multilinear'),
  step('2026-06','Dwivedi–Pago–Seppelt publish symmetric-model separations and new completeness results at STOC 2026; these do not separate unrestricted VP and VBP.','symmetry'),
  step('2026-06-12','The revised Bhargav–Dwivedi–Saxena survey retains VF⊆VBP⊆VP with no strict containment known, in Remark 7.3.','primer')],
 references=[
  ref('primer','A primer on the closure of algebraic complexity classes under factoring','C. S. Bhargav; Prateek Dwivedi; Nitin Saxena',2026,'https://eccc.weizmann.ac.il/report/2025/083/revision/1/download/','Revision 1, 12 June 2026; §4.5, Definitions 4.10–4.12 and Remark 7.3 on printed p. 53; original June 2025 question numbering has changed'),
  ref('depth','Homogeneous Algebraic Complexity Theory and Algebraic Formulas','Pranjal Dutta; Fulvio Gesmundo; Christian Ikenmeyer; Gorav Jindal; Vladimir Lysikov',2024,'https://drops.dagstuhl.de/entities/document/10.4230/LIPIcs.ITCS.2024.43','ITCS 2024, 43:1–43:23; §§2 and 6 review quasipolynomial equivalence; reference 35 is the classical depth-reduction theorem'),
  ref('factors','Factorization of Polynomials Given by Arithmetic Branching Programs','Amit Sinhababu; Thomas Thierauf',2021,'https://doi.org/10.1007/s00037-021-00215-0','Computational Complexity 30, article 15, published 15 October 2021; Theorem 4.1, characteristic-zero factor closure; CCC 2020 precursor'),
  ref('symmetry','Lower Bounds in Algebraic Complexity via Symmetry and Homomorphism Polynomials','Prateek Dwivedi; Benedikt Pago; Tim Seppelt',2026,'https://arxiv.org/abs/2601.09343','Preprint 14 January; STOC 2026, pp. 631–640, DOI 10.1145/3798129.3800779; introduction explains orbit-size symmetric classes, denoted symVS in the conference version'),
  ref('multilinear','Multilinear Algebraic Branching Programs and the Min-Partition Rank Method','Théo Fabris; Nutan Limaye; Srikanth Srinivasan; Amir Yehudayoff',2026,'https://eccc.weizmann.ac.il/report/2026/001/','ECCC TR26-001, 1 January; multilinear model and combinatorial characterization of the rank method’s potential')],
 status='source_open',status_note='Checked through 10 September 2026. The June 2026 survey explicitly leaves the VP–VBP inclusion unresolved. STOC 2026 symmetry separations, multilinear lower-bound methods and min-plus-semiring results use different models. This card fixes characteristic zero over ℂ, unrestricted commutative ABPs and exact nonuniform computation.',formulation_reviewed_on='2026-09-10')
